    {
      "id": "BN-C02-naam",
      "sequence": 70,
      "title": "নাম (nām) — \"name,\" a word older than Europe",
      "headword": "নাম",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "name",
      "script": "bengali",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:7b7ed7aa80c02555",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "The letters in this word"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called The letters in this word until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first word of the introduction — and one of the clearest bridges between India and Europe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ন (nô) + া (the long-ā sign) + ম (mô) becomes নাম (nām). Here the long-ā sign overrides the inherent ô, so it is nām, not \"nôm.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "নাম (nām, \"name\") from Sanskrit নামন্ (nāman), from Proto-Indo-European h₃nómn (its final n syllabic) — the same ancient root as English name, Latin nōmen (English noun, nominate), and Greek onoma. Bengali nām and English name are cousins from before either language existed — one of the clearest threads tying the Indian and European branches of one family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"nām\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"nām\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the family — nām / English name / Latin nōmen / Greek onoma",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the family — *nām* / English *name* / Latin *nōmen* / Greek *onoma*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What roots is nām from, and its English cousins? (Sanskrit nāman / PIE h₃nómn; name, noun, nominate.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "BN-C02-amar",
      "sequence": 80,
      "title": "আমার (āmār) — \"my\"",
      "headword": "আমার",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "my",
      "script": "bengali",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3b54acf2ae17b4b6",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "The letters in this word"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called The letters in this word until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The little word that makes the name yours."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "আ (the independent long ā, used because the word starts with a vowel) + মা (mā) + র (r) becomes আমার (āmār)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "আমার (āmār, \"my\") is the possessive of আমি (āmi, \"I\"), on the first-person stem ām- from Sanskrit asmad (the \"we/I\" stem). Buried in it is the same ancient first-person m- that runs through English me, my, mine and Latin mē — so Bengali's \"my\" and English's \"my\" share, at the root, one first-person sound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: no gender here",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Note something Bengali does not do: আমার never changes for gender. Hindi and Punjabi have merā (m.) / merī (f.); Bengali has just āmār, for a man or a woman, a masculine or feminine thing. Bengali threw grammatical gender away entirely — you will see this again and again, and it makes Bengali, in this one respect, simpler than its western cousins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"āmār\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"āmār\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "its owner (āmi, \"I\")",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: its owner (*āmi*, \"I\")]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "does it change for a man vs. a woman? (No — Bengali has no gender)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: does it change for a man vs. a woman? (No — Bengali has no gender)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is āmār the possessive of, and how does it differ from Hindi's merā? (Āmi, \"I\"; āmār never changes for gender, where merā/merī does.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "BN-C02-amar-naam",
      "sequence": 90,
      "title": "আমার নাম … (āmār nām …) — \"my name is…,\" with no \"is\"",
      "headword": "আমার নাম …",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "my name is… (with no \"is\")",
      "script": "bengali",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "sight-cue"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e858fa3fcf2976a1",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Assemble the two atoms — and notice a whole word English needs that Bengali drops."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "The two words — assembled",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "আমার (my) + নাম (name) + your name becomes আমার নাম … = \"my name …\" becomes \"my name is…\" Āmār nām Arun. becomes \"My name is Arun.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: Bengali drops \"is\" in the present",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at what is missing: there is no verb. Bengali sets \"my name\" beside \"Arun\" — āmār nām Arun — and the \"is\" is simply understood. This is the zero copula: for present-tense equational sentences (X is Y), Bengali needs no \"to be\" at all. (It is the same habit Tamil has — and unlike Hindi's merā nām … hai, which keeps the hai.) Bengali only brings back a verb when it talks about a state or place — you'll see that in Chapter 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"āmār nām …\" with your name",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"āmār nām …\" with your name]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "notice — no word for \"is\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: notice — no word for \"is\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Give your name in Bengali, and say what it leaves out that English requires. (Āmār nām ….; the verb \"is\" — the zero copula.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "BN-C02-alaap",
      "sequence": 100,
      "title": "আলাপ করে ভালো লাগলো (ālāp kore bhālo lāglo) — \"pleased to meet you\"",
      "headword": "আলাপ করে ভালো লাগলো",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "pleased to meet you (lit. \"having made acquaintance, [it] felt good\")",
      "script": "bengali",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:62a910da1c2e20d4",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "The letters in this word"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called The letters in this word until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The warm close of an introduction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The key word is আলাপ (ālāp): আ + লা (lā) + প (p)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "আলাপ করে ভালো লাগলো = আলাপ (ālāp, \"acquaintance, conversation\") + করে (kore, \"having done\") + ভালো লাগলো (bhālo lāglo, \"[it] felt good\") — literally \"having made your acquaintance, [it] felt good.\" The key word আলাপ (ālāp) is Sanskrit — ālāpa (\"conversing, addressing,\" from ā- \"toward\" + lap \"to speak\"), the same ālāp that names the slow opening section of a rāga in Indian classical music, where the musician \"converses\" with the notes. So even a hello leans on Sanskrit's word for the meeting of two voices."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"ālāp kore bhālo lāglo\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"ālāp kore bhālo lāglo\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what ālāp means, and where else you've heard it (acquaintance/conversation; the ālāp of a rāga)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what *ālāp* means, and where else you've heard it (acquaintance/conversation; the *ālāp* of a rāga)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does the phrase literally say, and what is ālāp? (\"Having made [your] acquaintance, it felt good\"; Sanskrit ālāpa, \"conversing\" — as in a rāga's opening.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "BN-C02-ki",
      "sequence": 110,
      "title": "কি (ki) — \"what\"",
      "headword": "কি",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "what",
      "script": "bengali",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
